WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.061
The nursing crisis.

00:00:02.011 --> 00:00:04.500
When there's no one left to care.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.147
Canada needs sixty thousand or more additional nurses.

00:00:08.147 --> 00:00:15.329
Emergency departments across rural and small-town Canada have closed or reduced hours because there aren't enough nurses to staff them.

00:00:15.329 --> 00:00:20.215
Nurses are leaving the profession faster than nursing schools can graduate replacements.

00:00:20.215 --> 00:00:24.262
Burnout from the pandemic compounded a shortage that existed before COVID.

00:00:24.262 --> 00:00:34.249
Meanwhile, internationally educated nurses face a thirty percent first-attempt pass rate on Canadian licensing exams — the same credential trap documented in the immigration pipeline.

00:00:34.249 --> 00:00:37.863
The nursing crisis is not separate from the MAID pipeline.

00:00:37.863 --> 00:00:40.829
It is the staffing mechanism that produces it.

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:44.556
When there aren't enough nurses, patients don't get timely care.

00:00:44.556 --> 00:00:46.363
Conditions worsen.

00:00:46.363 --> 00:00:48.840
Suffering becomes irremediable.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.965
MAID becomes available.

00:00:50.965 --> 00:00:53.624
The shortage is the pipeline's engine.

00:00:53.624 --> 00:00:56.352
Three dimensions of the nursing crisis.

00:00:56.352 --> 00:00:59.499
How the nursing crisis feeds the MAID pipeline.

00:00:59.499 --> 00:01:02.045
The nursing crisis is the engine.

00:01:02.045 --> 00:01:05.534
Without nurses, the healthcare system cannot function.

00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:09.363
Without a functioning healthcare system, patients deteriorate.

00:01:09.363 --> 00:01:12.556
Deteriorating patients become MAID eligible.

00:01:12.556 --> 00:01:15.261
Each MAID death saves the system money.

00:01:15.261 --> 00:01:20.624
The Pearce Study quantifies the savings for fifteen point one million chronic condition patients.

00:01:20.624 --> 00:01:25.909
The nursing shortage is not an accident or an unavoidable consequence of demographics.

00:01:25.909 --> 00:01:35.795
It is the product of credential barriers that prevent qualified immigrants from practicing, wage conditions that drive existing nurses out, and training capacity that has not kept pace with demand.

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:39.556
Every dimension of the nursing crisis is a policy choice.

00:01:39.556 --> 00:01:44.499
Every policy choice maintains the conditions that produce MAID eligibility at scale.
